  "summary": "Label-free and fluorescence imaging paired with learned features now yield comparable per-cell phenotypes across populations and inferred trajectories.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the conversion of cellular imaging and single-cell trajectory data into quantities that can be compared across cells, conditions, and populations. A holotomography study pairs label-free imaging with AI-enabled subcellular segmentation to derive biophysical phenotypic fingerprints of hepatic stellate cell activation in live cells. A persistent-homology shape distance (PH distance) compares single-cell morphologies from fluorescence images and is benchmarked against aspect ratio. PB-scope, an unsupervised deep-learning imaging tool, classifies 280 compounds by processing-body features and implicates JAK signaling. Pseudotime graph diffusion, by contrast, applies random-walk smoothing to cell features along inferred trajectories to improve post hoc visualization. What is new is the shift from segmentation and feature extraction toward distance metrics, learned embeddings, and smoothing operators that make per-cell measurements comparable across populations and trajectories.",
